Unfortunately, JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a text-based data interchange format that represents structured data as key-value pairs. It does not have any built-in sorting functionality. However, you can sort the keys of a JavaScript object using the `Object.keys()` method in combination with `Array.sort()`. Here's an implementation:

```js
function sortJSONByKeys(obj) {
  const keys = Object.keys(obj);
  keys.sort((a, b) => {
    // Define the sort order based on your requirements
    // This implementation sorts alphabetically
    return a.localeCompare(b);
  });
  const sortedObj = {};
  for (let I = 0; I < keys.length; i++) {
    sortedObj[keys[i]] = obj[keys[i]];
  }
  return sortedObj;
}
```

This function takes an object as input and returns a new object with the same keys sorted in ascending order according to their names. The original object is not modified. The implementation uses the `Object.keys()` method to extract all the keys of the input object into an array, which is then sorted using the `Array.sort()` method. The `localeCompare()` method is used to compare two strings alphabetically, but other comparison functions could be used depending on your specific use case. Finally, a new object is created by iterating over the sorted keys and copying the corresponding values from the input object.

Note that this implementation assumes that there are no duplicate keys in the input object, since `Object.keys()` returns unique keys only. If duplicates are allowed or needed, you might want to modify the implementation accordingly.

